What is memory ordering :
=========================
Modern CPUs and compilers reorder instructions to improve performance.
Without synchronization, different threads may observe operations in a different order than the source code.

Memory ordering tells the compiler and CPU what reorderings are allowed.

1.  memory_order_relaxed : 
Only guarantees the operation itself is atomic. 
#include <atomic>
#include <thread>
#include <iostream>

std::atomic<int> counter(0);

void increment()
{
    for(int i=0;i<100000;i++)
        counter.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
}

int main()
{
    std::thread t1(increment);
    std::thread t2(increment);

    t1.join();
    t2.join();

    std::cout << counter << std::endl;
}

This works because you only care about the correctness of the counter value, not ordering with respect to other memory accesses. 


2. memory_order_release : 
Release is used by the thread publishing data. 
data = 100;

ready.store(true, std::memory_order_release);

Every write before the store() becomes visible before another thread successfully performs a matching acquire load.

3. memory_order_acquire 
Consumer: 
while(!ready.load(std::memory_order_acquire));

std::cout << data;
Once the acquire sees the released value, no subsequent reads can move before the acquire.

#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <atomic>

std::atomic<bool> ready(false);
int data = 0;

void producer()
{
    data = 100;

    ready.store(true, std::memory_order_release);
}

void consumer()
{
    while(!ready.load(std::memory_order_acquire));

    std::cout << data << std::endl;
}

int main()
{
    std::thread t1(producer);
    std::thread t2(consumer);

    t1.join();
    t2.join();
}

