compare and exchange :
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>
#include <atomic>

std::atomic<int> winner(0);

void worker(int id)
{
    int expected = 0;

    if (winner.compare_exchange_strong(expected, id))
        std::cout << "Thread "
                  << id
                  << " won\n";
}

int main()
{
    std::thread t1(worker,1);
    std::thread t2(worker,2);
    std::thread t3(worker,3);

    t1.join();
    t2.join();
    t3.join();
}

Fails only when the values differ.
Easier to understand.
Good for one-shot comparisons.

//compare excahnge weak 

May fail even when the values are equal (called a spurious failure).
Usually more efficient on some CPU architectures.
Intended to be used inside a retry loop.

Example:

while (!value.compare_exchange_weak(expected, desired))
{
    // retry
}

Q >> Why is compare_exchange_weak() usually used inside a loop?

Because it may fail due to:

>> Another thread modifying the value.
>> A spurious failure, even if the value has not changed.

Using a loop handles both cases correctly:

int expected = atomicValue.load();

while (!atomicValue.compare_exchange_weak(expected, expected + 1))
{
    // expected is automatically updated on each failure
}

This pattern is the standard building block for many lock-free algorithms.
>> compare_exchange_weak() can fail for two reasons. First, the atomic object's value may not match the expected value because another thread updated it. 
Second, it may fail spuriously even when the values match. Spurious failures are allowed by 
the C++ standard to support efficient implementations on architectures that use load-linked/store-conditional (LL/SC).
Therefore, compare_exchange_weak() is normally used inside a retry loop, while compare_exchange_strong() is preferred for single-attempt operations."
